// =============================================================================
// MASES UI — Node-side TypeScript configuration
// =============================================================================
//
// File:    mase_app/tsconfig.node.json
// Phase:   1 (Frontend foundation) — Unit 1.3
// Version: 1.0.1
// Status:  PROVISIONAL — the pinned TypeScript and @types/node majors
//          are confirmed by Phase 0 Unit 0.1 Part E.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Revision history
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.0.0  — initial delivery. Declared `composite: true` to satisfy the
//          `references` block in tsconfig.json (Unit 1.2 v1.0.0).
// 1.0.1  — `composite: true` removed. The `references` block that
//          required it was removed from tsconfig.json in the same
//          revision, because TypeScript 5.7 rejects `composite: true`
//          + `noEmit: true` on a referenced project (TS6310). With the
//          reference gone, `composite` has no purpose; removing it
//          restores `noEmit: true` to a standalone project, which is
//          universally valid. See the header comment §"Why no longer
//          composite".
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What this project covers
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Build-time Node code. Today: exactly one file.
//
//     vite.config.ts
//
// Future build-time scripts (e.g. a scripts/*.ts helper invoked by an
// npm script) belong here too. They are added to `include` below when
// they are created; the compilerOptions do not change.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why a separate project (the split rationale)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// tsconfig.json (Unit 1.2) governs browser code (src/, the two entry
// point .ts files). It declares:
//
//     lib:    ES2022, DOM, DOM.Iterable
//     types:  vite/client
//
// This project governs Node code. It declares:
//
//     types:  node
//
// Splitting them into two projects prevents two classes of defect:
//
//   1. Browser code accidentally relying on Node globals
//      (process.env.XXX in a component, Buffer in a service, etc.).
//      If tsconfig.json did not restrict `types`, these would compile
//      and fail at runtime in a way that is hard to trace.
//
//   2. Build-time code accidentally relying on browser globals
//      (document, window, localStorage) in vite.config.ts. This
//      project omits the DOM lib, so those references fail at
//      type-check time rather than producing a confusing runtime
//      error during the build.
//
// The split is the standard Vite template convention and mirrors the
// reviewer's Phase 1 Unit 1.3 specification.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why no longer composite
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The v1.0.0 file was composite because tsconfig.json (Unit 1.2
// v1.0.0) referenced it in a `references` block. TypeScript requires
// that a project participating in a reference declare `composite:
// true`.
//
// TypeScript 5.7 rejects the pairing `composite: true` + `noEmit:
// true` on a referenced project with:
//
//     error TS6310: Referenced project
//     '.../tsconfig.node.json' may not disable emit.
//
// Two resolutions existed:
//
//     (a) remove the reference from tsconfig.json and remove
//         `composite` here; or
//     (b) allow emit of declaration files into a temp directory
//         (`emitDeclarationOnly: true` + `outDir`).
//
// The v1.0.1 revision applies (a) — the file's own documented
// "simplest" option. Removing the reference also removes the reason
// `composite` was needed, so `composite` is removed in this file.
// `noEmit: true` on a standalone project is universally valid.
//
// IDE tooling still works: VS Code selects this tsconfig for
// vite.config.ts by this file's own `include: ["vite.config.ts"]`
// pattern, which does not depend on the project-reference mechanism.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What this project does NOT declare
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// · No `strict: true`.
//   The delivered vite.config.ts (Unit 1.5) is written without
//   implicit-any issues; it does not reference untyped values. Adding
//   `strict` here would surface diagnostics that are not architectural
//   defects. A future CR can add strict diagnostics to the build
//   config if the reviewer wants CI to enforce them — this file
//   deliberately leaves that as a per-CR decision.
//
// · No `paths` alias.
//   vite.config.ts uses relative paths and the `node:url` module. It
//   does not import from `@/`; that alias is for browser code.
//
// · No `lib`.
//   The default library for target ES2022 is sufficient and correct.
//   Declaring `lib` explicitly would either duplicate the default
//   (harmless) or accidentally widen it (a defect). Omitting it is
//   the cleanest expression of "no browser globals".
//
// · No `composite`.
//   See §"Why no longer composite" above.
//
// =============================================================================

{
  "compilerOptions": {
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Target and module system
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Ambient type packages — Node globals only
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Provides `process`, `Buffer`, `NodeJS.ProcessEnv`, and the module
    // resolution behavior for `node:*` imports. Required by
    // vite.config.ts's `import { fileURLToPath, URL } from 'node:url'`.
    //
    // The DOM library is deliberately not declared (neither via `lib`
    // nor via an ambient types entry). If vite.config.ts tried to use
    // `document` or `window`, the compiler would reject it.
    "types": ["node"],

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Interop and resolution
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Emit — none
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Standalone project, no reference consumer. `noEmit: true` is
    // valid on its own; see the header comment §"Why no longer
    // composite".
    "noEmit": true,

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Isolated modules
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Not strictly required for a single-file build script, but kept
    // consistent with tsconfig.json (Unit 1.2) so the two projects
    // express the same module-system contract.
    "isolatedModules": true
  },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Project contents
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Exactly the build-time configuration today. Future build scripts
  // (scripts/*.ts) are added to this list when they are created.
  "include": [
    "vite.config.ts"
  ]
}